# Coding rules

Child-readable code, adult-level production safety.

## This project

- Backend is Python and FastAPI. Install and run it only with uv (`uv add`, `uv sync`, `uv run`). Do not use pip or Poetry.
- Frontend is React and TypeScript. Install and run it only with npm. Do not use yarn or pnpm.
- Durable patient and note data lives in PostgreSQL.
- There is no login. Do not add authentication. If login is added later, enforce authentication and authorization in FastAPI. Never rely on the React app as the only check.
- The patient summary is a template function in the backend. Do not add an LLM, RAG, Socket.IO, Redis, email, or ImageKit unless the product requirements change.
- The summary is a readable chart summary, not a diagnosis or treatment recommendation. Say that in the UI.

## Simplicity

- Simplicity comes first.
- Code must be production quality, not throwaway demo code.
- Prefer readability over cleverness.
- One file has one clear job.
- Create as few files as possible. Reuse an existing file, component, or helper before adding one.
- Do not duplicate behavior that already exists.
- Do not split simple logic across many files just to look architectural.
- Keep related logic together when that makes the flow easier to follow.
- Use FRONTEND/VITE and BACKEND/FASTAPI. Do not reorganize a working layout without a strong reason.
- Make the smallest correct change. Understand the existing flow before editing it. Work file by file.
- Add a dependency only when the standard library or an existing dependency cannot do the job cleanly.

## Names and size

- Use clear, fully descriptive names for variables, functions, classes, files, routes, and components.
- Avoid vague names such as data, thing, temp, x, or stuff when a meaningful name exists.
- A function does one thing. A component does one thing.
- Separate responsibilities only when the split makes the code clearer.

## File headers and comments

Every important application file starts with WHAT, WHY, HOW, and IMPORTANT.

- WHAT — what the file does.
- WHY — why the file exists.
- HOW — how it works with the rest of the app.
- IMPORTANT — anything dangerous, security-sensitive, unusual, or easy to break.

Put that header on Python modules under BACKEND/FASTAPI/app, React source under FRONTEND/VITE/src, Dockerfiles, docker-compose.yml, and .env.example. Skip lockfiles and generated files.

Comments explain why something exists when the code already shows what it does. Do not add filler comments. Use short section headings when a file has more than one block of related code.

## Backend is the source of truth

- Important behavior is decided in FastAPI, not in the browser.
- Never trust client input because the form already validated it. Validate again with Pydantic.
- Do not commit secrets, database passwords, or tokens. Put them in environment variables. .env.example contains placeholders only.
- Handle expected errors on purpose. Return consistent errors: 422 for invalid input, 404 for a missing patient or note, and a clear 500 when the database fails. Do not hide failures with a bare except or an empty catch.
- Log startup failure, seed failure, database failure, and unexpected exceptions. Do not add a separate request-logging middleware.
- Design each important flow for invalid input, network failure, database failure, and a missing record.
- This pass has no automated test suite. Still implement those failure responses and check them while building.

## How a feature should read

Follow the path UI, request, route, Pydantic validation, database, response, UI.

The UI shows loading, empty, validation, not-found, and network-error states. It does not show success when the API failed.
